Write a Python 3 script `analyze.py` (standard library only, no network) for incident log analysis.

Log format. Each line is a JSON object: `{"ts": "<ts>", "service": "<service>", "level": "<LEVEL>", "status": <int>, "latency_ms": <int>, "message": "<text>"}`; `status` and `latency_ms` are present only on request lines. Example:
`{"ts": "2026-03-04T10:15:03Z", "service": "checkout", "level": "ERROR", "status": 503, "latency_ms": 231, "message": "upstream timeout"}`
Timestamps are UTC. Blank lines are ignored. A non-blank line is malformed if it is not a valid JSON object, lacks `ts`/`service`/`level`/`message`, has a timestamp that is not a valid `%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ` UTC time, or a level that is not one of DEBUG, INFO, WARN, ERROR. Malformed lines are skipped (and counted where the spec says so); the script must never crash on them. Lines may be out of order.

Usage: `python analyze.py LOGFILE GAP_SECONDS`. Per service, sort its ERROR-level lines by time (the file may be unsorted) and group consecutive ones into an incident while the gap to the previous ERROR line of that service is <= GAP_SECONDS (a larger gap starts a new incident). Report only incidents with at least 2 error lines.
